# Query Intent Differentiation Rule

When generating or updating fix pages, Cursor must ensure each page targets a **distinct user intent**.

Pages must not only differ by keywords or providers — they must solve a **different technical problem**.

---

# 1) Intent clarity

Each page must clearly answer:

• What specific error or misconfiguration occurred  
• Why this error happens  
• What exact DNS change fixes it  

The explanation must be tailored to the specific issue.

---

# 2) Avoid near-duplicate explanations

Cursor must NOT reuse identical paragraphs across many pages.

Content must adapt to:

- provider context (Google, Microsoft, etc.)
- DNS host differences (Cloudflare, GoDaddy, etc.)
- specific SPF/DKIM/DMARC error condition

---

# 3) Distinct problem framing

Each page should frame the issue differently, for example:

- configuration missing
- syntax error
- provider alignment issue
- lookup limits
- selector problems
- DNS propagation delays

This ensures pages represent **different troubleshooting scenarios**.

---

# 4) Unique opening explanation

The first 120–150 words of every page must be unique and explain:

• what the error means  
• what usually causes it  
• why it matters for deliverability

This helps search engines recognize the page as **unique documentation**, not a doorway variation.

---

# 5) Structural consistency is allowed

Pages may share the same layout structure:

- One-Minute Fix
- Explanation
- Common mistakes
- Next steps
- Related fixes

But the **technical explanation inside each section must vary**.

---

# Goal

Ensure programmatic pages behave like **technical documentation pages**, not doorway pages generated from templates.